Title: Consensus Regions from Replicated ChIP-seq and ATAC-seq
        Experiments
Description: Merges the evidence carried by replicated ChIP-seq,
        ATAC-seq or CUT&RUN experiments into a single set of consensus
        regions. When several replicates agree on a position their
        p-values are combined, so that a peak too weak to survive on
        its own can still be retained. Fisher, Stouffer, Lancaster and
        rank-product combinations are available; the weighted ones
        accept per-replicate weights taken from FRiP scores, library
        sizes, or the peak sets themselves when no alignment file is at
        hand. Input can be anything from a full narrowPeak to a bare
        BED3: scores are rank-transformed when p-values are missing,
        and a weighted k-of-n presence rule takes over when even the
        score is absent. The threshold on the combined significance can
        be calibrated against a permutation null rather than fixed a
        priori. Quality control plots and export to
        SummarizedExperiment are included. The approach follows MSPC
        (Jalili et al., 2015). It differs from consensus sets built on
        occupancy, such as the minOverlap rule of DiffBind, in that a
        region is kept on the combined significance of the peaks that
        overlap it rather than on how many replicates called it, and
        from IDR, which ranks a pair of replicates by reproducibility,
        in that any number of replicates is reduced to one set of
        regions.
biocViews: ChIPSeq, ATACSeq, PeakDetection, Epigenetics, Sequencing,
