Title: Omics Feature Selection and Multiple Data Integration
Description: Explore how samples differ, which features vary together
        within and across omics data sets, and which combinations
        distinguish groups of samples. Our methods are omics agnostic
        and can apply in diverse fields of biology. Our multivariate
        models summarise many measurements in a few components, and our
        sparse models select small sets of features as candidate
        molecular signatures. When several types of omics data are
        measured on the same samples, DIABLO integrates those
        measurements. When independent studies measure the same
        variables, MINT combines them while accounting for batch
        effects between studies. MINT Block (NP-integration, with
        optional discriminant analysis and variable selection) brings
        both designs together, letting researchers ask whether a
        signature spanning molecular layers distinguishes groups across
        cohorts. Further methods support repeated measurements and
        compositional data. Sample plots, loadings and networks show
        how samples group, which features contribute to components and
        how features are associated. Cross-validation helps tune models
        and estimate predictive performance, and fitted models can
        predict outcomes for new samples.
